# Homework 3 -- Bounded infinite sets have a limit point

Using the concept of open covers (and explicitly avoiding the
Bolzano–Weierstrass theorem), prove that every bounded infinite set has a limit
point.

**Course material.**

- Lecture 5: [open and closed sets](../index.html#lecture05) (Abbott §3.2),
  Lecture 6: [compactness](../index.html#lecture06) (Abbott §3.3).
- Formalisation: [`theories/Topology.v`](../theories/Topology.v) (open sets
  `A is _open_`, from the Waterproof library `Waterproof.Libs.Analysis.OpenAndClosed`,
  and limit points `x is a _limit point_ of A`, i.e. `is_limit_point A x`) and
  [`theories/Compactness.v`](../theories/Compactness.v) (`open_cover`,
  `has_finite_subcover`, `compact_iff_finite_subcover`).

**The idea of the proof.** Let $A$ be bounded, say $A \subseteq [-M, M]$, and
suppose $A$ has *no* limit point. Then every $c \in [-M,M]$ has a radius
$r_c > 0$ such that the ball $(c - r_c, c + r_c)$ meets $A$ at most in $c$
itself. These balls form an open cover of $[-M,M]$, so by Heine–Borel finitely
many of them already cover $[-M,M]$ — and each of them contains at most one
point of $A$. Hence $A$ is finite, a contradiction.

Notes:

- You may use the Heine–Borel theorem (Lecture 6): it appears below as the
  hypothesis `HHB` of the main theorem, in the "every open cover of $[-M,M]$ has
  a finite subcover" form. You may **not** use Bolzano–Weierstrass.
- The cover is indexed by *pairs* (centre, radius): choosing one radius per
  centre would require the axiom of choice, and carrying the radius in the index
  avoids it. The "at most one point of $A$" condition is built into the set
  `iso_ball A p` below, so that it is empty for a bad choice of radius (and the
  empty set is open).

